; ============================================================================
; AIF-087 M2c -- BEFORE-trigger veto arm, THE WORK
;
; Status: CANDIDATE / explicit-run, driven from C++ by run_trigger_veto_arm.
; Safety: MUTATING (SANDBOX only; the arm erases the fixture when it finishes)
;
; RUN TWICE, UNCHANGED. The C++ arm runs this once with NO trigger registered
; (the CONTROL pass) and once with a refusing BEFORE callback (the VETO pass).
; Two markers are expected to FLIP between the passes and one is expected to
; stay the same:
;
;   TRG_W0  same in both  -- the fixture was really built and really opened.
;   TRG_W1  .T. control, .F. veto -- did the UPDATE reach the record?
;   TRG_W2  .F. control, .T. veto -- a successful COMMIT DELETES the journal
;                                    (journal_note_commit), so a journal that
;                                    SURVIVES is the refusal's fingerprint.
;
; W0 is this arm's control marker and it is not decoration. "Nothing was
; written" is also what you observe when the table never opened or the WHERE
; matched no row, so W1 and W2 mean NOTHING unless W0 is .T. in both passes.
; Never credit W1/W2 without W0.
; ============================================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/SANDBOX

FORMULA "TRGVETO-WORK-BEGIN"

SELECT 1
USE TRGVETO
GO 1
? "TRG_W0_fixture_row1_is_alpha:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "ALPHA")

; The single act under test. Autocommit SQLsel DML: begin_dml_scope forces
; buffering on, stages the change, and commits through the house cmd_COMMIT --
; which is where the BEFORE phase now fires.
SQLSEL UPDATE TRGVETO SET NAME = 'VETOED' WHERE ID = 1

GO 1
? "TRG_W1_row1_took_the_write:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "VETOED")
? "TRG_W2_journal_survived:" + FILE("DBF/SANDBOX/TRGVETO.dbf.tbj")

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TRG_W3 -- AIF-159 s3. A NATIVE COMMIT MUST NOT REACH AROUND THE SQL SCOPE.
;
; On the VETO pass the refused autocommit leaves the SQLsel transaction ACTIVE
; while the session is still in NATIVE mode. That window is the ONLY way to
; reach a native COMMIT with a live scope -- SQLSEL BEGIN refuses outside SQL
; mode, and SET MODE refuses to leave SQL mode with a transaction open -- and
; it is the window this marker stands in.
;
; BEFORE THE GUARD, the COMMIT below COMMITTED THE WRITE THE TRIGGER HAD JUST
; REFUSED, then left the scope open so every later autocommit staged instead of
; committing. So this marker reads .F. on a pre-guard binary and .T. after: it
; is a discriminator, not a decoration.
;
; Same line in both passes, as the whole arm requires. On the CONTROL pass no
; transaction is active and the buffer is empty, so COMMIT is a silent no-op and
; the row still reads VETOED from its own successful commit -- .F. there, and
; that .F. is asserted too, so a guard that refused EVERY native COMMIT would
; fail the control rather than pass unnoticed.
COMMIT
GO 1
? "TRG_W3_native_commit_did_not_write:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "ALPHA")

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; TRG_W4 -- AIF-159 s7.6. THE SAME DOOR, THE OTHER VERB.
;
; A native ROLLBACK had the identical blindness and a worse consequence: it
; DISCARDED the buffered changes and still left the scope open. Read that
; against TRG_T1 in the teardown, which retries the SAME buffered change and
; requires it to land -- if this ROLLBACK threw the buffer away, TRG_T1 goes
; RED. THAT is the discriminator for this half, and it is stated here because
; W4 ALONE IS WEAK: a rollback does not change committed truth, so the row
; reads ALPHA on the veto pass whether the verb was refused or obeyed.
;
; W4 is still worth its line. It is the CONTROL-side assertion -- .F. there,
; where no transaction is active and the row holds its own committed VETOED --
; so a guard that refused EVERY native ROLLBACK fails the control instead of
; passing quietly. The refusal itself is asserted by text on the veto pass.
ROLLBACK
GO 1
? "TRG_W4_native_rollback_left_the_row:" + (ALLTRIM(NAME) = "ALPHA")

; DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CLOSE. On the veto pass the buffer is RETAINED by
; design, and CLOSE ALL over a retained buffer raises the interactive
; "COMMIT changes? (y/N)" prompt -- which would hang the arm forever waiting
; for a human. The arm reads the journal here, then runs the teardown script.
FORMULA "TRGVETO-WORK-END"
